## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical IPC with shared memory and demonstrates mastery of memory ordering in a real, implementable primitive.

## Key Concepts
- Single-producer/single-consumer lock-free design
- Atomic operations and acquire/release semantics
- Memory visibility across cores and cache-line padding
- mmap-based IPC and data layout in shared memory

## Code Example
```c
// Simplified sketch (not production-ready)
typedef struct {
  uint64_t head; // read index
  uint64_t tail; // write index
  char data[4096];
} ring_t;

void prod(ring_t *r, const void *src, size_t n){
  uint64_t t = __atomic_load_n(&r->tail, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
  // write data to slot (omitting slot indexing details)
  memcpy(r->data + (t % 1024) * 4, src, n);
  __atomic_store_n(&r->tail, t+1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
}

void cons(ring_t *r, void *dst, size_t n){
  uint64_t t = __atomic_load_n(&r->tail, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE);
  uint64_t h = __atomic_load_n(&r->head, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
  if (h < t){
    memcpy(dst, r->data + (h % 1024) * 4, n);
    __atomic_store_n(&r->head, h+1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for multiple producers? discuss ring partitioning or a per-slot sequence counter.
- What changes for varying message sizes or overflow handling? discuss padding and data-copy costs.

## Why This Is Asked
Exposes real-world lock-free design pitfalls: memory ordering, ABA, and visibility in a high-throughput ring buffer.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free data structures and CAS
- Per-slot sequencing to avoid ABA
- Memory ordering on x86_64 vs ARM64
- Correctness under contention and wrap-around

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal enqueue (producer)
bool enqueue(slot_t* buf, int n, uint64_t key, uint64_t val) {
  static thread_local uint64_t prod = 0; // per-producer sequence
  uint64_t idx = atomic_fetch_add(&head, 1) % n;
  slot_t* s = &buf[idx];
  s->key = key;
  s->val = val;
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, prod*2+1, memory_order_release);
  prod++;
  return true;
}

// Minimal dequeue (consumer)
bool dequeue(slot_t* buf, int n, uint64_t* out_key, uint64_t* out_val) {
  static uint64_t tail = 0;
  slot_t* s = &buf[tail % n];
  uint64_t seq = atomic_load_acquire(&s->seq);
  if ((seq & 1) == 0) return false; // empty
  *out_key = s->key;
  *out_val = s->val;
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, seq+1, memory_order_release);
  tail++;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to multiple consumer threads?
- How to test for ABA and late-arriving writes under high contention?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of lock-free data structures, memory ordering semantics, and cross-platform memory barrier requirements in a production system context.

## Key Concepts
- MPMC ring buffer with per-slot sequence numbers
- ABA problem mitigation via publish/subscribe pattern
- Architecture-specific memory barrier requirements
- Contention handling and wraparound correctness

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-code for enqueue/dequeue operations
bool enqueue(struct ring *r, void *data) {
    size_t pos = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    size_t slot = pos % r->capacity;

    // Wait for slot to be available
    while (r->slots[slot].seq != pos) {
        // Backoff or handle contention
    }

    // Write payload
    r->slots[slot].data = data;

    // Publish with release barrier
    atomic_store_explicit(&r->slots[slot].seq, pos + 1, memory_order_release);
    return true;
}

bool dequeue(struct ring *r, void **data) {
    size_t pos = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_relaxed);
    size_t slot = pos % r->capacity;

    // Check if slot is ready with acquire barrier
    size_t seq = atomic_load_explicit(&r->slots[slot].seq, memory_order_acquire);
    if (seq != pos + 1) return false;

    // Read payload
    *data = r->slots[slot].data;

    // Advance head
    atomic_store_explicit(&r->head, pos + 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    return true;
}
```

## Memory Barriers by Architecture
- **x86_64**: `memory_order_release`/`memory_order_acquire` (no additional barriers needed)
- **ARM64**: `dmb ish` for release, `dmb ishld` for acquire operations

